## Chapter II New exponential law of \(\beta\)-decay half-lives of nuclei far from stability

In this chapter, we present the systematical study on new law and formula of \(\beta\)-decay half-lives for nuclear far from stability [106, 107, 108] and the parameterized formula to calculate half-lives of allowed orbital electron-capture transition. As discussed in section 1.1.3, the empirical study can help to understand the underlying physics of \(\beta\)-decay half-lives of nuclei far from \(\beta\)-stable line in an approximate but simple and transparent way. With the simple and accurate formula, it is also helpful for the calculations and predictions of \(\beta\)-decay half-lives of nuclei far from \(\beta\)-stable line, which is a prerequisite for a better understanding of the nucleosynthesis process.

In section 2.1, we propose an exponential law between \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives and the neutron numbers of parent nuclei far from the \(\beta\)-stable line. The underlying physics of the exponential law is discussed in section 2.2. In sections 2.3 and 2.4, new formulae are proposed for calculating nuclear \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives far from stability. The nuclear structure effects on \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives are also discussed. In section 2.5, we suggest a parameterized formula to calculate half-lives of allowed orbital electron-capture transition. The summary of this chapter is given in section 2.6.

### 2.1 New exponential law between \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives and neutron numbers

#### 2.1.1 Common behavior of \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives

There exists common character of \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives. For nuclei close to the \(\beta\)-stable line, dramatic variations exist in nuclear \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives. This is because the \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay energy of the nuclei close to the \(\beta\)-stable line is very small, and the nuclear structure effects, such as the even-odd effects and the structure of excited energy level, cause the large fluctuations of \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay energy (\(Q\) values). For small \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay \(Q\) values, the \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives are very sensitive to the \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay energy. Therefore the \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives often change dramatically along the isotopes close to \(\beta\)-stable line. For nuclei far from the \(\beta\)-stable line, the \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay energy is much larger than that of the nuclei close to the \(\beta\)-stable line. As a result, the influence of certain nuclear structureeffects can be smooth according to Fermi theory of \(\beta\)-decay. For example, we make a rough estimation of the effect of the decay energy on \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-life. For allowed \(\beta^{-}\)-transition, the \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-life (\(T_{1/2}^{\beta^{-}}\)) is proportional to the reciprocal of the Fermi integral function \(f(Z_{\rm d},W_{0})\)[109], which is written as

\[f(Z_{\rm d},W_{0})=\int_{1}^{W_{0}}F(Z_{\rm d},W)(W^{2}-1)^{1/2}(W_{0}-W)^{2}W{ \rm d}W, \tag{2.1}\]

where \(Z_{\rm d}\) is the proton number of the decay product, \(F(Z_{\rm d},W)\) is the factor of Coulomb correction, and W is the ratio between the total energy (including the rest energy) of the emitting electron and the rest mass of the electron. We assume that the factor of Coulomb correction is 1, and decay energy difference between two nuclei is 0.5 MeV. If the decay energy of one nuclide is 0.5 MeV, and that of the other is 1.0 Mev, the ratio \(f(Z_{\rm d},W_{\rm latt.})/f(Z_{\rm d},W_{\rm prev.})\) is 15.2, where \(f(Z_{\rm d},W_{\rm latt.})\) is Fermi integral function of the latter nuclide and \(f(Z_{\rm d},W_{\rm prev.})\) is that of the previous nuclei. As a result, the \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-life of the previous nuclide will be larger than that of the latter by 14.2 times when the matrix element of the two nuclei is the same. In the same way, if the decay energy of one nuclide is 10.0 MeV, and that of the other is 10.5 MeV, the ratio between \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-life of the previous nuclide and that of the latter will be 1.28 according to the Sargent law [14]. Similar argument is valid for \(\beta^{+}\) decay. This shows that the \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-life of nuclide with large \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay energy is less sensitive to

Figure 2.1: The large figure plots the \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives (in log\({}_{10}\)-scale) along Cs isotopic chain. The small figure in the inset shows the linear relationship between the logarithm of \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-life and the neutron number of Cs isotopes far from the \(\beta\)-stable line.

the change of decay \(Q\) values than the nuclide with small \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay energy for allowed \(\beta^{\pm}\)-transition. For nuclei far from the \(\beta\)-stable line, usually it is reasonable to assume that allowed \(\beta^{\pm}\)-transition exists because of the large \(\beta\)-decay energy and dense energy levels of daughter nuclei. Comparing with allowed transitions, the contributions from forbidden transitions are relatively small [13]. Therefore there may exist some rules of \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives for nuclei far from the \(\beta\)-stable line because of the large \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay energy. In Fig. 2.1 we plot the variation of \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-life with the neutron number of parent nuclei along Cs isotopic chain. It is seen from the large figure of Fig. 2.1 that there are large fluctuations of \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives of nuclei close to the \(\beta\)-stable line, whereas for nuclei far from the \(\beta\)-stable line (\(N\geq 88\) for neutron-rich Cs isotopes), there is linear relationship between \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-life and the neutron number of parent nuclei. The linear relationship is clearly shown in the small figure of Fig. 2.1.

At first, we define the concept "far from the \(\beta\)-stable line" used in this chapter quantitively. All available nuclei with \(\delta N>5\) are included in our investigation. The parameter \(\delta N=|N-N_{stable}|\) is defined to denote the nuclei far from the \(\beta\)-stable line. For the nuclei with even proton numbers, \(N_{stable}\)[110] is the maximum (for \(\beta^{-}\) decay) or minimum (for \(\beta^{+}\) decay) neutron number of corresponding stable nuclei. For odd-Z nuclei, we set \(N_{stable}\) as the average of the two maximum (for \(\beta^{-}\) decay) or minimum (for \(\beta^{+}\) decay) neutron numbers of two neighboring even-Z nuclei because the maximum (for \(\beta^{-}\) decay) or minimum (for \(\beta^{+}\) decay) neutron number of stable odd-Z nuclei is often smaller (for \(\beta^{-}\) decay) or larger (for \(\beta^{+}\) decay) than that of the neighboring stable even-Z nuclei. For nuclei with \(Z\geq 83\), there does not exist stable nuclei according to our

