present knowledge. We obtain \(N_{stable}\) from the semi-empirical formula [111] for nuclei with \(Z\geq 83\), which is written as

\[N_{stable}=A-\frac{A}{1.98+0.0155A^{2/3}}, \tag{2.2}\]

where A is nuclear mass number.

#### \(\beta^{+}\) decays

Usually, one use the comparative half-lives other than the \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-life to classify the order of \(\beta^{+}\)-decay in textbooks [2]. Although the \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives approximately vary from \(10^{-2}\)s to \(10^{18}\)y for known nuclei, the comparative half-lives vary in a very narrow range. For example, the comparative half-lives (in log\({}_{10}\)-scale) of the super allowed transitions change from 2.9 to 3.7 for many nuclei [111].

Beyond the traditional method based on nuclear \(\beta\)-decays close to \(\beta\)-stable line, we directly analyze the relationship between the experimental \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives (in log\({}_{10}\)-scale) and the neutron number of parent nuclei. At first, we investigate the \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives of the nuclei near the closed shells, because there usually exist more experimental data of the half-lives of \(\beta^{+}\)-decay with a same order near the closed shells. All the experimental values (without special notation) of \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives which are used in this thesis are taken from Nubase table of nuclear and decay properties by Audi _et al._[112]. The \(\beta^{+}\)-decay in the Nubase table [112] contains two kinds of process: positron emission and orbital electron capture. The variations of the logarithms of \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives along different isotopic chains near the closed shells are shown in Figs. 2.2\(-\)2.4. For a few isotopic chains, the available experimental data of \(\beta^{+}\)-decay with a

