same order is less than 3. In this case, we do not draw them in the figures. For example, the isotopic chains of K, Co, and Cd are not drawn in Figs. 2.2\(-\)2.4. A few points are missing in the figures because there is no experimental data for the \(\beta^{+}\)-decay of a few nuclei.

From Figs. 2.2\(-\)2.4 we find that the logarithms of half-lives of \(\beta^{+}\)-decay with a same order approximately lie on a straight line along any isotopic chain near the proton closed shells (Z=20, 28, 50, 82). This linear relationship between the logarithm of half-life and the neutron number can be written as

\[\log_{10}T_{1/2}=aN+b, \tag{2.3}\]

where \(a\) and \(b\) are two constants to be determined and \(N\) is the neutron number of the nuclei on the isotopic chain. This equation shows an exponential law between the \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives and the neutron number of parent nuclei. The dashed lines in Figs. 2.2\(-\)2.4 are plotted according to the linear fit of the experimental \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives (in \(\log_{10}\)-scale). The standard deviation of the linear fit is defined as

\[\sigma=\sqrt{\frac{1}{K}\sum_{i=1}^{K}[\log_{10}T_{1/2}^{i}(\exp)-\log_{10}T_{ 1/2}^{i}(\mbox{cal})]^{2}}. \tag{2.4}\]

Here, K is the number of experimental data of the nuclei with the same order of \(\beta^{+}\)-decay on an isotopic chain, \(T_{1/2}^{i}(\exp)\) and \(T_{1/2}^{i}(\mbox{cal})\) represent the experimental half-life value and the calculated one respectively.

