#### 2.3.3 Even-odd effects on \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives

We now discuss the e-o effects on \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives. For the allowed \(\beta^{+}\)-decay, the calculated \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives by this formula is greatly improved by taking into account the e-o effects (\(\sigma\) decreases from 0.70 to 0.47). The differences between \(c_{4}^{\rm ee}\) and \(c_{4}^{\rm oo}\) are close to 1.5 (see Table 2.7). This shows that the \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives of even-even nuclei are averagely larger than those of the odd-odd nuclei by about 30 times for allowed \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions. Therefore the e-o effect is very apparent for the allowed \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions. Whereas for the first and second forbidden \(\beta^{+}\) decays, the improvement on the calculated results is not so apparent as the allowed \(\beta^{+}\)-transition after taking into the e-o effects (\(\sigma\) decreases from 0.36 to 0.35 and from 0.39 to 0.35, respectively 

[MISSING_PAGE_EMPTY:71]

for the first and second forbidden \(\beta^{+}\) decays). The differences between \(c_{4}^{\rm ee}\) and \(c_{4}^{\rm oo}\) are 0.24 and 0.51, respectively for the first and second forbidden \(\beta^{+}\) decays (see Table 2.7). This denotes that the \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives of the even-even nuclei are averagely larger than those of the odd-odd nuclei by 0.7 and 2.2 times for the first and second forbidden \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions, respectively. Therefore the e-o effects on the half-lives of the first and second forbidden \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions are much less apparent than those on the half-lives of allowed \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions.

It is interesting to analyze the reason which causes the difference of the e-o effects on the half-lives of allowed and of forbidden \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions. It is known that there are \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions from the ground states of parent nuclei to excited levels of daughter nuclei. For allowed \(\beta^{+}\)-decay, the branching ratio of the \(\beta^{+}\)-transition from ground state to ground state is relatively larger than that of the \(\beta^{+}\)-transition from ground state to excited state for many nuclei because the decay energy of \(\beta^{+}\)-transition from ground state to ground state is the largest and many \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions from ground state to excited states are forbidden transitions. For example, the branching ratios of the \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions from ground states to ground states for \({}^{124}\)Cs, \({}^{125}\)Cs, and \({}^{126}\)Cs are 58%, 54%, and 56.4% [126], respectively. For most nuclei close to the \(\beta\)-stable line, the branching ratios of the \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions from ground states to ground states are close to 100%, such as \({}^{130}\)Cs and \({}^{131}\)Cs, with the branching ratios 94.5% and 100% [126], respectively. The available energy of the \(\beta^{+}\)-transition (from ground state to ground state) of the even-even nuclei is smaller than that of the corresponding odd-odd nuclei because of the effects of pairing energy. As a result, the \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives of the even-even nuclei are larger than that of the corresponding odd-odd nuclei. Therefore, the e-o effects are apparent for the nuclei with mainly allowed \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions from ground state to ground state.

For the forbidden \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions, the \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions from ground states to ground states are not the main decay channel for many nuclei because there usually exists the allowed \(\beta^{+}\)-transition from ground state to the excited energy level of daughter nuclei. For example, the branching ratios of the \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions from ground states to ground states for \({}^{200}\)Tl, \({}^{190}\)Pb, \({}^{192}\)Pb, and \({}^{194}\)Pb are 5.8%, 0.5%, 0.5%, and 0.8% [126], respectively. For the \(\beta^{+}\)-transition from ground state to excited state, the Q-value of the decay can be written as

\[Q_{oo}^{e.x.}=Q_{oo}^{g.s.}-E_{ee}^{e.x.},\qquad\mbox{for odd-odd ~{}nuclei},\] \[Q_{ee}^{e.x.}=Q_{ee}^{g.s.}-E_{oo}^{e.x.},\qquad\mbox{for even-even ~{}nuclei}, \tag{2.16}\]where \(Q^{e.x.}\) and \(Q^{g.s.}\) is the Q-value of the \(\beta^{+}\)-transition from ground state to exited state and of the \(\beta^{+}\)-transition from ground state to ground state, respectively. \(E^{e.x.}\) is the excited energy of the daughter nuclei. The excited energy of even-even nuclei is often larger than that of the odd-odd nuclei, that is, \(E^{e.x.}_{ee}>E^{e.x.}_{oo}\). Although \(Q^{g.s.}_{oo}\) is larger than \(Q^{g.s.}_{ee}\) due to the effect of pairing energy, \(E^{e.x.}_{ee}\) is larger than \(E^{e.x.}_{oo}\). As a result, the difference between \(Q^{e.x.}_{oo}\) and \(Q^{e.x.}_{ee}\) decreases. In other words, the difference between the excited energy of even-even nuclei and that of the odd-odd nuclei partially offsets the effect of pairing energy for forbidden \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions. Therefore the e-o effects on the half-lives of the forbidden \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions are less apparent than those on the half-lives of the allowed \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions.

### 2.4 New formula for \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives

#### 2.4.1 New formula for \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives of nuclei far from stable stability

Since the half-lives of \(\beta^{-}\) decay and \(\beta^{+}\) decay obey a similar exponential law for nuclei far from \(\beta\)-stability, we use the same formula which is justified for the \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives to describe the \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives. The formula is written as

\[\log_{10}T_{1/2}=(c_{1}Z+c_{2})N+c_{3}Z+c_{4}, \tag{2.17}\]

where \(T_{1/2}\) is the half-life of \(\beta^{-}\)-decay (in seconds) and \(Z\) is the proton number of parent nuclei. The values of parameters \(c_{1}\), \(c_{2}\), \(c_{3}\) and \(c_{4}\) are determined according to all available experimental data of \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives of nuclei far from the \(\beta\)-stable line (\(\delta N>5\)). Through a least-square fit to all available experimental data of 351 nuclei far from the \(\beta\)-stable line, we obtain a set of parameters of Eq. (2.17): \(c_{1}=2.65\times 10^{-4}\), \(c_{2}=-0.2275\), \(c_{3}=0.3652\), and \(c_{4}=-0.8852\). The standard deviation \(\sigma\) and average deviation \(\Delta\) between the logarithms of the calculated and experimental \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives are used to denote the reliability of the formula. The standard deviation for the 351 nuclei is 0.44 (a factor of 2.8). The average deviation which is written as

\[\Delta=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{i=1}^{K}|\log_{10}T_{\rm cal}^{i}-\log_{10}T_{\rm exp}^ {i}| \tag{2.18}\]

is 0.35 (a factor of 2.2). It is seen from Figs. (2.18a) and (2.18b) that experimental \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives of nuclei far from \(\beta\)-stable line are reproduced reasonably by this simple formula [Eq. (2.17)] with four parameters.

When we focus on Figs. (2.18a) and (2.18b), we find that the calculated \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives are systematically smaller than the experimental ones for nuclei near the proton "magic numbers" (\(Z=20,28,50,82\)) or the neutron "magic numbers" (\(N=28,50,82,126\)). This indicates that the nuclei near the proton or neutron shells are stabler than other nuclei despite they are far from the \(\beta\)-stable line. The shell effects still strongly influence \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives of nuclei far from stability. For example, near the "magic cross" at \({}^{132}\)Sn, the experimental \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives are underestimated by about 2 orders of magnitudes from Eq. (2.17). The simple formula [Eq. (2.17)] only reflects the average trends of the change of \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives for nuclei far from stability. Therefore the shell corrections which is caused by the quantum effect should

Figure 2.18: The ratios \(T_{\rm cal}/T_{\rm exp}\) (in log\({}_{10}\)-scale) between calculated and experimental \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives plotted versus proton and neutron numbers of parent nuclei. Deviation by a factor of 3 between theory and experiment is represented by broken lines. The figure shows three sets of numerical results: Eq. (2.17) (Form.1), Eq. (2.21) (Form.2), and Eq. (2.22) (Form.3).

be included.

The details of the shell effects on \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives will be discussed in section 2.4.2. Here we take into account the shell effects empirically. It is seen from Figs. (2.18a) and (2.18b) that the closer the nuclei to the proton or neutron "magic-number" regions, the larger deviations exist between calculated \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives and experimental ones. There seems to exist a correlation between \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives and the fine details in the systematics of the ground masses of nuclei. A simple way of exhibiting this correlation is to include the deviations between experimental ground state masses and theoretical ones from the smooth liquid drop model (\(\Delta M=M_{\rm exp}-M_{\rm the}\), in units of MeV/\(c^{2}\)) in Eq. (2.17). The function of shell correction is empirically written as

\[shell(Z,N,\Delta M,Q_{\beta^{-}})=c_{5}\frac{\Delta Mc^{2}}{Q_{\beta^{-}}+c_{6 }ZN}, \tag{2.19}\]

where \(Q_{\beta^{-}}\) is the \(Q\) values of \(\beta^{-}\)-decay (in units of MeV), and \(c_{5}\) and \(c_{6}\) are two constants to be determined. The denominator of Eq. (2.19) demonstrates the hindrance factor caused by \(\Delta M\). In our calculations, the experimental ground state mass \(M_{\rm exp}\) are taken from the 2003 atomic mass table by Audi _et al._[116]. The semiempirical mass surface \(M_{\rm the}\) is \(M_{\rm the}=ZM(^{1}H)+NM(n)-B/c^{2}\), where \(M(^{1}H)\) and \(M(n)\) are experimental masses of hydrogen and neutron, respectively. \(B\) is the binding energy from the liquid drop model [127]:

\[B=15.73A-17.77A^{2/3}-0.70905\frac{Z^{2}}{A^{1/3}}-(26.46A-17.70A^{2/3})I^{2}, \tag{2.20}\]

where \(I=(N-Z)/A\) is the _charge-asymmetry parameter_ of the given nucleus [127].

After including the shell effects, we have the following formula

\[\log_{10}T_{1/2}=(c_{1}Z+c_{2})N+c_{3}Z+c_{4}+c_{5}\frac{\Delta Mc^{2}}{Q_{ \beta^{-}}+c_{6}ZN} \tag{2.21}\]

to describe the \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives. According to a least square fit to available experimental data, we obtain a set of parameters of Eq. (2.21): \(c_{1}=3.77\times 10^{-4}\), \(c_{2}=-0.2990\), \(c_{3}=0.4629\), \(c_{4}=-0.5230\), \(c_{5}=-1.0222\), and \(c_{6}=1.16\times 10^{-3}\). The standard deviation \(\sigma\) for the 351 nuclei is 0.34 (a factor of 2.19), and the average deviation \(\Delta\) is 0.28 (a factor of 1.91).

In order to use the formula to calculate \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives of nuclei with unknown masses, we consider another form to describe the shell effects. The Gaussian function is used to characterize the shell effects of a single "magic number" region and the effects of "magic cross" region near \({}^{132}\)Sn on \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives. The functions of shell corrections and \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives are written as

\[shell(Z,N)=c_{5}[e^{-(N-29)^{2}/15}+e^{-(N-50)^{2}/37}+e^{-(N-85)^{2 }/9}\] \[+e^{-(N-131)^{2}/3}]+c_{6}e^{-[(Z-51.5)^{2}+(N-80.5)^{2}]/1.9},\] \[\log_{10}T_{1/2}=(c_{1}Z+c_{2})N+c_{3}Z+c_{4}+shell(Z,N). \tag{2.22}\]

The particular values used in the Gaussian functions of Eq. (2.22) are empirically determined according to detailed analysis of Figs. (2.18a) and (2.18b). The chosen centers of Gauss functions are close to the "magic number" region and sometimes it slightly deviates from the "magic number" because of the dramatic changes of the \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives and energy level structures of daughter nuclei close to the "magic number" and "magic cross" regions. For example, to describe the \(Z=50\) and \(N=82\) "magic cross" region, we use the term \(c_{6}e^{-[(Z-51.5)^{2}+(N-80.5)^{2}]/1.9}\) plus the term \(c_{5}e^{-(N-85)^{2}/9}\). The sum of the two terms makes shell correction on \(N=80\) bigger than that on \(N=82\) for Sn isotopic chain. This reason is that \({}^{130}\)Sn is relatively closer to \(\beta^{-}\)-stable line than \({}^{132}\)Sn. According to our analysis in section 2.1.1, the \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-life of the nuclide close to \(\beta\)-stable line is more sensitive to nuclear structure effects than that of the nuclide far from the \(\beta\)-stable line. As a result, the shell correction on \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-life of \({}^{130}\)Sn is relatively larger than that of \({}^{132}\)Sn. And the choice of the particular value of sigma used for Gauss function is based on the width of influenced nuclear region by corresponding "magic number". For example, we can see from Fig. 2.18(b) that the influencing region of \(N=50\) closed shell are approximately from \(N=44\) to \(N=56\), therefore we choose the term \(c_{5}e^{-(N-50)^{2}/37}\) (sigma is close to 6) to describe the shell correction of \(N=50\) closed shell.

The parameters of Eq. (2.22) are obtained through a least-square fit to the experimental data: \(c_{1}=3.37\times 10^{-4}\), \(c_{2}=-0.2558\), \(c_{3}=0.4028\), \(c_{4}=-1.0100\), \(c_{5}=0.9039\), and \(c_{6}=7.7139\). The standard deviation \(\sigma\) is 0.27 (a factor of 1.86), and the average deviation \(\Delta\) is 0.21 (a factor of 1.62). The ratio (in \(\log_{10}\)-scale) between calculated half-life and experimental one is plotted versus proton number (left panel) and neutron number (right panel) of parent nucleus in Fig. 2.18. It is seen from Figs. (2.18c), (2.18d), (2.18e), and (2.18f) that the ratios between calculated \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives and experimental ones are within a factor of 3 for most nuclei, indicating good agreement with the experimental data. Form.3 reproduces experimental data better than Form.2. It reproduces 261 (74.4%) nuclei of all available experimental data of 351 nuclei within a factor of 2, and 324 (92.3%) nuclei within a factor of 3. The calculated results from Form.3 [Eq. (2.22)] is comparable to those calculated from the microscopic model. For example, the calculation using pn-QRPA model by Nabi _et al._[13] reproduces 82.2% of all experimentally known \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives shorter than one minute within a factor of 2, and 90.8% within a factor of 3 in the mass range \(A=40-100\). In addition, the large changes of nucleon number (A=17\(-\)238) and large changes of \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives (\(10^{-3}\)\(-\)\(10^{3}\) s) in our calculation demand that the formula has a firm basis in physics. It may reflect the statistical behavior of nuclear \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives due to the fact that the \(\beta^{-}\)-decays occur between the ground state of parent nucleus to multi-energy levels of daughter nucleus for nuclei far from \(\beta\)-stable line. Therefore, we can draw the conclusion that the formula is valid for describing \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives of nuclei far from the \(\beta\)-stable line.

