The good agreement between calculated half-lives and experimental ones shows that the formula of allowed EC-decay half-lives has a firm basis in physics. By taking into account the hindrance of angular momentum on nuclear matrix elements, important physics is included in the formula. The parameterized formula is not only simple, but also practical to experimentalists for estimating EC-decay half-lives of nuclei conveniently. A typical example is the Viola-Seaborg formula [120, 121] and Geiger-Nuttall law, which are widely used in the calculation of \(\alpha\)-decay half-lives [120, 121, 125]. It is plotted in Fig. 2.22 the variation of experimental nuclear matrix element of allowed EC transition (\(\log_{10}|M_{\rm exp}|-c_{1}Z-h_{F}-h_{S}\)) with angular momentum of parent and daughter nuclei (\(\sqrt{j_{p}(j_{p}+1)j_{d}(j_{d}+1)}\)\(\hbar^{2}\)). We can see clearly from Fig. 2.22 that experimental dots scatter around a descendent straight line, which shows the hindrance of angular momentum on nuclear matrix elements of allowed EC transition.

Figure 2.22: Variation of experimental nuclear matrix element of allowed EC transition (\(\log_{10}|M_{\rm exp}|-c_{1}Z-h_{F}-h_{S}\)) with angular momentum of parent and daughter nuclei (\(\sqrt{j_{p}(j_{p}+1)j_{d}(j_{d}+1)}\)\(\hbar^{2}\)).

The hindrance of angular momentum on nuclear matrix elements seems similar to unfavored \(\alpha\)-decay. The starting point of microscopic theory of \(\alpha\)-decay is the basic quantum mechanical expression of perturbation rate [2]

\[\lambda=\frac{2\pi}{\hbar}|H_{\rm if}|^{2}\frac{{\rm d}n}{{\rm d}E}, \tag{2.31}\]

where \(H_{\rm if}\) is the transition perturbation matrix element and \({\rm d}n/{\rm d}E\) is the density of final states. The favored \(\alpha\)-decay is S-state decay with a zero orbital angular momentum of the relative movement between \(\alpha\) particle and daughter nuclei. However, in unfavored \(\alpha\)-decay, there exists non-zero orbital angular momentum. The wave function of non-zero orbital angular momentum is different from S-state wave function and may cause hindrance on the matrix element of \(\alpha\)-decay. It is shown that there exists similar behavior for the half-lives of orbital electron capture and \(\alpha\)-decay although they are governed by different interactions, that is, the angular hindrance on transition matrix elements due to the overlap of wave function of initial state and final state.

#### 2.5.3 Orbital electron capture of uranium and transuranic nuclei

\(\alpha\)-decay, \(\beta^{+}\)-decay, and spontaneous fission are main decay modes of proton-rich heavy and superheavy nuclei. Up till now, \(\alpha\)-decay and spontaneous fission are observed in most of synthesized superheavy nuclei. For the nuclei approaching \(\beta\)-stable line, the competition of orbital electron capture (EC) with \(\alpha\)-decay and spontaneous fission becomes larger than that of the nuclei far from \(\beta\)-stability [28, 29]. Therefore it is necessary to study the orbital electron-capture rates of heavy and superheavy nuclei. This is especially helpful to experimental physicists for designing experiments to identify the synthesized superheavy nuclei approaching the superheavy stable island [27, 28, 29] according to their decay properties. We use the new formula [Eq. (2.28)] to calculate the half-lives of allowed EC transitions of U and transuranic nuclei. For U and transuranic nuclei, orbital electron capture dominates the \(\beta^{+}\)-decay process (including \(e^{+}\)-emission and EC process) in the region of low decay energy because the ratio \((\lambda_{K}/\lambda_{e^{+}})\) between orbital electron K-capture rate and positron emission is approximately proportion to \(Z^{3}\). If \(Q<6\) MeV and \(Z>90\), the ratio \(\lambda_{K}/\lambda_{e^{+}}\) is larger than 10. The decay \(Q\) values are smaller than 6 MeV for most of known \(\beta^{+}\)-decay of U and transuranic nuclei. Therefore we approximately replace the experimental half-life of electron capture by that of \(\beta^{+}\)-decay in the Nubase Table [112] when we compare calculated results with experimental ones. Squared amplitudes of bound-state electron radial wave functions

