### Summary of this chapter

In summary, we systematically investigate the experimental data of \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives for both light nuclei and heavy nuclei far from \(\beta\)-stable line. A new exponential law is found between the half-life of \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay and the nucleon number (Z, N) for proton-rich or neutron-rich nuclei. This new law implies the exponential dependence of nuclear \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives on the maximum kinetic energy of the electron or positron emitted in the \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay process. It reflects the statistical properties of multi-energy-level decay behavior for nucleus far from \(\beta\)-stable line, that is, from the ground state of parent nucleus to many excited energy levels of daughter nucleus. This is superior to the Sargent law, a fifth power law between \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives and decay energies, which can be approximately derived from single-level Fermi theory of \(\beta\)-decay.

Based on the new exponential law and including reasonable nuclear structure effects, new formulae are proposed to describe the \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives of nuclei far from \(\beta\)-stableline. Experimental half-lives are well reproduced by the simple formulae. The agreement with experimental data is comparable or even slightly better than the best microscopic model calculations for 735 nuclei ranging from \(A=9-238\) and with half-lives spanning 6 orders of magnitude (\(10^{-3}\)\(-\)\(10^{3}\) s). This shows that the formula have a firm basis in physics. It can be used to predict the \(\beta^{\pm}\)-decay half-lives of the nuclei far from the \(\beta\)-stable line.

Interesting nuclear structure effects on \(\beta\)-decay half-lives manifest beyond the "mean" behavior part of the simple formula (after systematical analysis, the part of nuclear structure effects is also included in our formula). For example, it is noticed that the even-odd effects on the \(\beta^{+}\)-decay half-lives of the first and second forbidden \(\beta^{+}\) decays are much less apparent than those of the allowed \(\beta^{+}\)-transition. This is a new phenomenon and it demonstrates that the allowed \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions are more sensitive to nuclear even-odd effect than the forbidden \(\beta^{+}\)-transitions. It is also found that the shell or subshell effect will strongly affect \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives even for nuclei far from \(\beta\)-stable line. The additional correlation between the major shell or subshell closures and \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives is obtained according to systematic investigation of nuclear \(\beta^{-}\)-decay half-lives. This correlation provides the feasibility of obtaining information of the major shell or subshell closures of the nuclei far from the \(\beta\)-stable line according to the exponential law.

Besides the above study for nuclei far from stability, the half-life of allowed orbital electron-capture (EC) transition of the nucleus close to \(\beta\)-stable line is also systematically studied. We have found the hindrance of angular momentum of parent and daughter nuclei on half-life of allowed orbital electron-capture (EC) transition. The hindrance of angular momentum is related to the overlap between multi-particle wave functions of parent and daughter nuclei. A new parameterized formula including the effect of angular momentum hindrance is proposed to calculate the half-life of allowed EC transition of the nucleus close to \(\beta\)-stable line. Experimental half-lives of allowed EC transitions of nuclei close to \(\beta\)-stable line are well reproduced by this formula. In addition, the formula is also proved to be valid for calculating allowed EC-decay half-lives of U and transuranic nuclei. It is useful to experimental physicists for designing experiments to identify the synthesized long-lived superheavy nuclei approaching the superheavy stable island according to their decay properties and analyzing experimental data.

## Chapter 3 \(\alpha\) decays and spontaneous fissions of heavy and superheavy nuclei

(\alpha\) decay half-lives of ground and isomeric states calculated in a unified theoretical framework

The quantum theory of \(\alpha\)-decay was established in 1928 by Gamow according to tunnelling through a potential barrier [148]. From then on, various models [149, 150, 151, 152, 153, 154, 155, 156, 157, 158] are developed to study the favored \(\alpha\) decays occurring between the ground states of nuclei. For a long time the theoretical research on \(\alpha\) decay concentrated on the transitions from ground-states to ground-states and the model of the decay was built based on the data of nuclei in ground states. It is known that there are isomers in nuclei and some isomers have \(\alpha\)-decays. The first isomer \({}^{234}\)Pa\({}^{m}\) was found as early as 1921 in the \({}^{234}\)Th \(\beta^{-}\) decay [111]. Along with the recent development of experimental technology, many new isomers have been discovered and have formed the island of isomerism near the proton or neutron shells. \(\alpha\) transitions from isomeric states to ground states or to isomeric states are found from many nuclei. Very recently, it is reported from the \(\alpha\)-decay data of \({}^{270}\)Ds and \({}^{271}\)Ds that there are isomeric states in \({}^{270}\)Ds and \({}^{271}\)Ds [159, 27]. \(\alpha\) decay from the isomeric state of \({}^{270}\)Ds was observed with a half-life of \(6.0^{+8.2}_{-2.2}ms\) that is significantly longer than the half-life \(100^{+140}_{-40}\mu s\) of the corresponding ground state [159, 160]. Configuration-constrained calculations of potential-energy surfaces in even-even superheavy nuclei demonstrated that there existed enhanced stability of superheavy nuclei due to high-spin isomerism [160].

Although many \(\alpha\)-decays from isomers are observed experimentally, theoretical studies on them are rare. Up to now, there are few systematic theoretical calculations on the \(\alpha\)-decay half-lives of isomers as far as we know. This may be due to the fact that the isomers found long time ago are not \(\alpha\)-emitters. It is not clear whether \(\alpha\)-decay models of the ground states are valid for \(\alpha\)-decays of isomers. Therefore a systematical research on the \(\alpha\)-decay half-lives of isomeric-states is necessary. It can help us to check the validity of the ground-state \(\alpha\)-decay model for the decays of isomeric-states, to calculate the \(\alpha\)-decay half-lives of isomeric states and to predict some long-lived superheavy elements.

The effective liquid drop model was established in 1993 by Goncalves and Duarte for describing the \(\alpha\) decay and cluster radioactivity in a unified framework [161, 162]. Systematic calculation on \(\alpha\) transitions from ground states to ground states using different combinations of mass transfer descriptions and inertia coefficients showed its validity in reproducing the experimental data [163]. As a fission-like model, it has been extended to deal with proton emission and cold fission process. Here we generalize it to calculate the \(\alpha\)-decay half-lives of isomeric states.

The aim of this part is to extend the effective liquid drop model to calculate the \(\alpha\) transitions from isomeric-states to ground-states and from isomeric-states to isomeric-states [164, 125]. Comparisons between the experimental data and calculated half-lives have been performed for the \(\alpha\) decays occurring between the ground states of even-even heavy and superheavy nuclei (Z\(\geq\)100), for the favored \(\alpha\) decays of isomeric-states near Z=82 shell and for the \(\alpha\) decays of isomers in heavy nuclei (Z\(\geq\)99). In this part, we introduce the effective liquid drop model in section 3.1.1. The calculations of \(\alpha\)-decay half-lives and comparisons with experimental data are presented in sections 3.1.2, 3.1.3, and 3.1.4. A summary of this part is given in section 3.1.5.

#### The effective liquid drop model

In the effective liquid drop model, \(\alpha\) decay process is assumed as a super asymmetric fission. The dinuclear shape (see Fig. 3.1) is described as two intersecting spheres.

Figure 3.1: Schematic representation of the dinuclear decaying system. The daughter nucleus and the emitted (smaller) fragment have radius \(R_{2}\) and \(R_{1}\), respectively, and the distance between the geometrical centers of the fragments is denoted by \(\zeta\). The variable \(\xi\) represents the distance between the center of the heavier fragment and the circular sharp neck of radius \(a\).

Four independent collective coordinates imported to determine the configuration of the nascent fragments are as follows: the radii of each spherical fragment, \(R_{1}\) and \(R_{2}\), respectively for the \(\alpha\) particle and daughter nucleus; the distance between their geometric centers, \(\zeta\); and the height of the larger spherical segment, \(\xi\). Three constraints are introduced to reduce the spherical four-dimensional problem to the equivalent one-dimensional one [162]. The incompressibility of nuclear matter demands that the total volume of the dinuclear system is constant, i.e.

\[2(R_{1}^{3}+R_{2}^{3})+3[R_{1}^{2}(\zeta-\xi)+R_{2}^{2}\xi]-[( \zeta-\xi)^{3}+\xi^{3}]=4R_{\rm p}^{3}, \tag{3.1}\]

where \(R_{\rm p}\) is the radius of the parent nucleus.

To keep the two spherical segments in contact, it is necessary to establish a geometric constraint,

\[R_{1}^{2}-(\zeta-\xi)^{2}=R_{2}^{2}-\xi^{2}. \tag{3.2}\]

In the constant mass asymmetry shape description (CMAS), the volume of each fragment is set to be constant, and in terms of the lighter fragment the volume conservation gives

\[2R_{1}^{3}+3R_{1}^{2}(\zeta-\xi)-(\zeta-\xi)^{3}-4\bar{R}_{1}^{3} =0, \tag{3.3}\]

where \(\bar{R}_{1}\) is the final radius of the \(\alpha\) fragment.

There are three important contributions to the potential energy of the dinuclear shape: the Coulomb energy, effective surface energy and the centrifugal potential energy [162]. The effective, one-dimensional total potential energy is given by

\[V=V_{\rm c}+V_{\rm s}+V_{l}. \tag{3.4}\]

Coulomb contribution \(V_{\rm c}\) is determined by using an analytical solution of Poisson's equation for a uniform charge distribution system [165]. Goncalves _et al._[161] defined a effective surface tension \(\sigma_{\rm eff}\) to calculate the effective surface potential of a nuclear liquid. \(\sigma_{\rm eff}\) is defined as a function of the the proton numbers of the parent (\(Z_{\rm p}\)), emitted (\(Z_{1}\)), and daughter (\(Z_{2}\)) nucleus, reads

\[\sigma_{\rm eff}=\frac{1}{4\pi(R_{\rm p}^{2}-\bar{R}_{1}^{2}-\bar{R}_{2}^{2})} [Q-\frac{3}{20\pi\varepsilon_{0}}e^{2}(\frac{Z_{\rm p}^{2}}{R_{\rm p}}-\frac{ Z_{1}^{2}}{\bar{R}_{1}}-\frac{Z_{2}^{2}}{\bar{R}_{2}})], \tag{3.5}\]

where \(\bar{R}_{2}\) is the final radius of the daughter fragment. The Q value which denotes the energy released in the \(\alpha\) decay process is taken from the 2003 atomic mass evaluation by Audi _et al._[112, 116]. Therefore, the effective surface energy can be calculated as

\[V_{\rm s}=\sigma_{\rm eff}(S_{1}+S_{2}), \tag{3.6}\]

where \(S_{1}\) and \(S_{2}\) are the surface areas of each spherical segment.

The centrifugal potential energy after the scission point reads

\[V_{l}=\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2\bar{\mu}}\frac{l(l+1)}{\zeta^{2}}, \tag{3.7}\]

Where \(l\) is the angular momentum of \(\alpha\) particle, \(\bar{\mu}=M_{1}M_{2}/(M_{1}+M_{2})\) is the reduced mass of two separated nuclei and \(M_{\rm i}\) (\({\rm i}=1,2\)) represents their atomic masses.

The effective mass before scission point is described by effective inertia coefficient [162] and given by the following equation

\[\mu_{\rm eff}=\bar{\mu}\alpha^{2}, \tag{3.8}\]

with

\[\alpha=1+\frac{\pi}{v_{1}}[R_{1}^{2}-(\zeta-\xi)^{2}][R_{1}\frac {{\rm d}R_{1}}{{\rm d}\zeta}-(\zeta-\xi)(1-\frac{{\rm d}\xi}{{\rm d}\zeta})]\] \[+\frac{\pi}{v_{2}}(R_{2}^{2}-\xi^{2})(R_{2}\frac{{\rm d}R_{2}}{{ \rm d}\zeta}-\xi\frac{{\rm d}\xi}{{\rm d}\zeta}). \tag{3.9}\]

The penetrability factor is calculated by

\[\Gamma=\exp[-\frac{2}{\hbar}\int_{\zeta_{0}}^{\zeta_{c}}\sqrt{2\mu[V(\zeta)-Q] }\ {\rm d}\zeta], \tag{3.10}\]

where \(\zeta_{0}\) and \(\zeta_{c}\) are the inner and outer classical turning points. Finally, the half-life is calculated as

\[\tau=\frac{\ln 2}{\lambda_{0}\Gamma}, \tag{3.11}\]

where \(\lambda_{0}\) (with the dimension s\({}^{-1}\)) is a parameter associated with the time scale of \(\alpha\) particle preformation and the surface oscillation characteristic time of the parent nucleus [162]. \(\lambda_{0}\) together with the radius parameter, \(r_{0}\), is determined in order to obtain the best agreement with experimental data [162].

#### 3.1.2 \(\alpha\) decays between the ground states of nuclei

Tavares and co-workers have calculated the favored \(\alpha\) transitions from ground-states to ground-states in a wide region of nuclei and have reproduced the experimental data well [163]. Here we test this model for heavy nuclei (Z\(\geq 82\)) before the calculation of \(\alpha\)-decays of isomers. In the calculation, we choose the same model parameters (\(r_{0}=1.13\)\(\lambda_{0}=1.8\times 10^{22}\) s\({}^{-1}\)) for the combination of constant mass asymmetry description and effective inertia coefficient, as Tavares _et al._ did in reference [163]. The calculated \(\alpha\)-decay half-lives agree with experimental data well. As an example, we list the calculated half-lives of the even-even heavy and superheavy nuclei in table 3.1. In table 3.1, the first and second columns mark the proton number and mass number of the parent nucleus, respectively. The \(\alpha\) decay energy, experimental half-lives and theoretical ones are given in columns 3, 4 and 5. The ratio between theoretical half-life and experimental one is listed in the last column.

The parent nuclei given in table 3.1 belong to recently observed \(\alpha\) decays. It is seen from table 3.1 that theoretical half-lives are close to experimental ones, within a factor of 2\(-\)3. It is seen clearly from Fig. 3.2 that the ratio (\(T_{\rm cal}/T_{\rm exp}\)) lies between two broken lines 0.5 and 1.5. There is a little bigger deviation for the two nuclei \({}^{256}\)Fm and \({}^{266}\)Sg, with the factors of 2 and 3 respectively. It is interesting to see (in Fig. 3.3) that \(\alpha\)-decay half-lives of the nuclei with the neutron number N=152 are longer than those of the neighboring ones, while \(\alpha\)-decay half-lives of the nuclei with the neutron number N=154 are shorter than those of the neighboring ones. This phenomenon demonstrates that there are subshell effects for the neutron number N=152. From Fig. 3.3 we can also see the good agreement between calculated values and experimental data.

