form a new island of isomerism before the shell closure. \(\alpha\) decays have been found in more than 28 isomers in the region. This is because most of the synthesized superheavy nuclei are proton-rich. As a result, large branching ratios of \(\alpha\) decay and \(\beta^{+}\) decay are observed in these isomers.

We extend the model which has been proved efficient for the favored \(\alpha\)-decays of both the ground-states and isomer-states to calculate the \(\alpha\)-decay half-lives of heavy and superheavy isomers. For the unfavored \(\alpha\)-decays of isomeric-states, we take into account the effect of centrifugal potential barrier in the calculation. Numerical results and experimental data are listed in Table 3.3. \(L_{\alpha}\) is the angular momentum of \(\alpha\) particle.

