and is undesirable since it requires the centrality cuts to change as a function of \(V_{z}\). Another variable is proposed called \(gRefMult\) which counts global tracks under the following conditions: \(|\eta|<0.5\), \(dca<3\) and \(nHitsFit\geq 10\). After extensive tests, \(gRefMult\)'s reconstruction efficiency was determined to be stable as function of \(V_{z}\) and running time. Subsequent fit with a MC Glauber soft+hard simulation to the high end of the \(grefmult\) distribution results in the centrality cuts which are listed in Table 5.3.

The remaining issues are biases on multiplicity distribution introduced by the main online VPD trigger setup [217]. The biases come from two sources. Firstly, over the full range in \(V_{z}\), the VPD is more efficient at triggering on central events relative to peripheral. This leads to a general deficit in peripheral events for a given data sample. The second comes from a centrality dependence of the VPD's online \(V_{z}\) resolution which is worse for peripheral events relative to central due to lower multiplicity in peripheral events. Since the trigger-setup insisted events fall within the inner tracking acceptance, i.e., with an online cut of \(|V_{z}(\mbox{VPD})|<5\) cm, the resolution issue means that events at the higher "real" primary vertex Z (PVz, from TPC tracking, much better resolution) are more likely to be peripheral whereas the events at lower PVz are more likely to be central. This is shown in Figs. 5.1(a) and 5.1(c).

The \(V_{z}\) dependent biases in multiplicity distribution require a re-weighting correction to be applied for the analysis. For our analysis with a "signal" summed up over a range of \(gRefMult\), events at PVz\(\sim\)0 will have their peripheral contribution scaled up in order to restore the unbiased case via the correction. The opposite will be true for events at higher \(|\mbox{PVz}|\) where the peripheral contribution will be scaled down - again to restore the unbiased case. The correction has to be applied as a function of PVz in 2 cm bins for acceptance reasons. The typical weight factor is shown in Figs. 5.1(b) and 5.1(d). Detailed documentation on Run 7 Au+Au 200 GeV centr

