Raw yields of \(\phi\) mesons are determined by fitting the background subtracted \(m_{\rm inv}\) distribution in the appropriate range with a Breit-Wigner function superimposed on a linear (or polynomial) background function

\[\frac{dN}{dm_{inv}}=\frac{A\Gamma}{(m_{inv}-m_{0})^{2}+\Gamma^{2}/4}+B(m_{ inv}), \tag{5.2}\]

where \(A\) is the area under the peak corresponding to the number of \(\phi\)-mesons, \(\Gamma\) is the Full Width at Half Maximum (FWHM) of the peak, \(m_{0}\) is the resonance mass position. \(B(m_{inv})\) denotes a linear (\(B(m_{inv})=p_{0}+p_{1}m_{inv}\)) (Fig. 5.4(c)), or polynomial (\(B(m_{inv})=p_{0}+p_{1}m_{inv}+p_{2}m_{inv}^{2}\)) (Fig. 5.4(d)) residual background function. The parameters (\(p_{0}\), \(p_{1}\) and \(p_{2}\)) of \(B(m_{inv})\) are free parameters.

Due to limited momentum resolution of the measured particles, it will propagate to the mass measurement. The observed \(\phi\) meson width distributions usually show an apparent FWHM larger than the value from Particle Data Group (PDG) [220], being a convolution with a resolution function due to measurement uncertainties. Previous STAR \(\phi\) meson raw yields were extracted from standard Breit-

