Table 5.6: Selection criteria for flow tracks used in the event plane reconstruction plane is reconstructed in each sub-event, denoted as \(\Psi_{2}^{a}\) and \(\Psi_{2}^{b}\). Then the event plane resolution \(r=\cos[2(\Psi_{2}-\Psi_{\rm rp})]\) can be calculated from Eq.(14) and (11) from [52], which are shown as follows:

\[<\cos[2(\Psi_{2}-\Psi_{\rm rp})]>=\frac{\pi}{2\sqrt{2}}\chi_{2}\exp(-\chi_{2}^{ 2}/4)\times[I_{0}(\chi_{2}^{2}/4)+I_{1}(\chi_{2}^{2}/4)], \tag{5.5}\]

\[<\cos[2(\Psi_{2}^{a}-\Psi_{\rm rp})]>=\sqrt{<\cos[2(\Psi_{2}^{a}-\Psi_{2}^{b})] >}, \tag{5.6}\]

where \(\chi_{2}\equiv v_{2}/\sigma=(v_{2}\sqrt{2N}\) for number flow) and \(I_{1}\) is the first order modified Bessel function. Firstly, we obtain the sub-event resolution \(<\cos[2(\Psi_{2}^{a}-\Psi_{\rm rp})]>\) from Eq. (5.6). Then using Eq. (5.5) applied on sub-event, we can extract the sub-event \(\chi_{2}^{a}\). The variable is proportional to \(\sqrt{N}\), so the total event \(\chi_{2}\) was obtained by \(\chi_{2}=\sqrt{2}\chi_{2}^{a}\). And after putting this \(\chi_{2}\) into Eq. (5.5), we can calculate the final full event resolution. The physical \(v_{2}\) is calculated as \(v_{2}=v_{2}^{obs}/r\). However, experimentally, what we observe is \(<v_{2}^{obs}>\), the averaged \(v_{2}^{obs}\) over a data sample (particle wise), such as the minimum bias events. Then

\[<v_{2}>=<\frac{v_{2}^{obs}}{r}>\approx({\rm but}\ \neq)\frac{<v_{2}^{obs}>}{<r>}. \tag{5.7}\]

This is not quite correct when we just divide the \(<v_{2}^{obs}>\) by the event-wise averaged resolution \(<r>\). Practically, we calculate a track-wise averaged resolution by weighting each event with the number of observed particles that used in \(v_{2}^{obs}\) calculation. As shown in Fig. 4.3 of Ref. [222], there is small difference between event-wise averaged and track-wise averaged event plane resolution for peripheral centrality bins. Fig. 5.11 presents the reaction plane resolutions in different centrality bins for Au+Au 200 GeV collisions. The minimum bias resolution (0-80%, centrality -1 in Fig. 5.11) are weighted by \(\phi\) meson raw yields.

\(v_{2}^{obs}\) of \(\phi\) meson is extracted by the invariant mass method. The method was proposed in Ref. [223], which decomposes the anisotropic flow (\(v_{n}\)) of short-lived particle from that of all possible daughter pairs as a function of invariant mass. For extracting the \(v_{2}\) of \(\phi\) meson, it utilizes the fact that the \(v_{2}\) of \(K^{+}K^{-}\) pairs is composed of the \(v_{2}\) of combinatorial background and the \(v_{2}\) of \(\phi\) meson. Following the mixed event background subtraction procedure described in section 5.3.2, the number of \(K^{+}K^{-}\) pairs in each invariant-mass bin are counted, irrespective of the pair azimuth. Then

\[N_{K^{+}K^{-}}(m_{\rm inv})=N_{\phi}(m_{\rm inv})+N_{B}(m_{\rm inv}), \tag{5.8}\]where \(N_{\phi}\) and \(N_{B}\) are from \(\phi\) meson signal and the background respectively. Once \(N_{\phi}\) has been extracted via event-mixing and subsequent fitting of \(\phi\) meson invariant mass distribution with Eq. (5.2) for each \(p_{T}\) bin (see section 5.3.2), \(N_{B}\) can be obtained from Eq. (5.8). It is shown in Fig. 5.12 the \(\phi\) meson invariant mass peaks after subtracting the mixed-event background at different \(p_{T}\) for Run 7 Au+Au 200 GeV collisions (0-80%). The invariant mass peak after background subtraction is prominent and can be well described by the Breit-Wigner + linear/polynomial background function. From the fitting, we can get \(N_{\phi}(m_{\rm inv})\) and \(N_{B}(m_{\rm inv})\).

The same-event \(v_{2}\) for \(K^{+}K^{-}\) pairs versus invariant mass can be described by the equation [211]

\[v_{2}(m_{\rm inv})=a(m_{\rm inv})v_{2S}+[1-a(m_{\rm inv})]v_{2B}(m_{\rm inv}), \tag{5.9}\]

where \(v_{2}(m_{\rm inv})\) is the \(v_{2}\) of same-event \(K^{+}K^{-}\) pairs, \(v_{2S}\equiv v_{2\phi}\) is the \(v_{2}\) of the \(\phi\) meson, \(v_{2B}\) is the effective \(v_{2}\) of the combinatorial background and \(a(m_{\rm inv})=N_{\phi}(m_{\rm inv})/N_{K^{+}K^{-}}(m_{\rm inv})\) is the ratio of \(\phi\) signal to the sum of background and \(\phi\) signal. The reaction plane angle, \(\Psi_{2}\), was estimated according to Eq. (5.4). \(v_{2}(m_{\rm inv})\) can then be calculated from the following equation for each \(v_{2}(m_{\rm inv})\) bin,

\[v_{2}(m_{\rm inv})=<\cos[2(\varphi_{KK}-\Psi_{2})]>, \tag{5.10}\]

Figure 5.11: Event plane (\(\Psi_{2}\)) resolution as a function of centrality (centralities 1 to 9 are from peripheral (70-80%) to central collisions (0-5%) as defined in Table 5.3) in Run 7 Au+Au 200 GeV collisions. The minimum bias resolution (0-80%, centrality -1 in this plot) are weighted by \(\phi\) meson raw yields.

where \(\varphi_{KK}\) is the azimuthal angle of the \(K^{+}K^{-}\) pair.

Under the assumption that the background contribution to \(v_{2}(m_{\rm inv})\) (the second part on right side of Eq. (5.9)) is smooth as a function of \(m_{\rm inv}\)[223], a polynomial function, \(p_{0}+p_{1}m_{\rm inv}+p_{2}m_{\rm inv}^{2}+p_{3}m_{\rm inv}^{3}\), can be used to parameterize the background \(v_{2B}\) versus \(m_{\rm inv}\). \(v_{2S}\) was then obtained by fitting \(v_{2}(m_{\rm inv})\) by Eq. (5.9) in each \(p_{T}\) bin, with \(v_{2S}\) as a free parameter. Figure 5.13 shows \(<\cos[2(\varphi_{KK}-\Psi_{2})]>\) (that is, \(v_{2}(m_{\rm inv})\) in Eq. (5.10)) versus \(m_{\rm inv}\) at different \(p_{T}\) in Au+Au 200 GeV collisions (0-80%), where the solid curve is the result of fitting by Eq. (5.9). The decrease of \(<\cos[2(\varphi_{KK}-\Psi_{2})]>\) around \(\phi\) meson invariant peak (\(m_{\rm inv}=1.01946\) GeV/\(c^{2}\)) is due to relatively small \(\phi\) meson \(v_{2}\) compared to the \(v_{2}\) of background \(K^{+}K^{-}\) pairs in the same \(p_{T}\) range. Besides the \(v_{2}\) difference between \(v_{2\phi}\) and \(v_{2B}\), the signal to background ratio also strongly affects the uncertainty of \(v_{2\phi}\) extraction (i.e., the error bar \(\sigma(v_{2\phi})\propto 1/a(m_{\rm inv})\)). At the same time, \(<\sin[2(\varphi_{KK}-\Psi_{2})]>\) vs. \(m_{\rm inv}\) is found, as expected, to be consistent with zero

Figure 5.12: \(\phi\) meson invariant mass peaks after subtracting the mixed-event background at different \(p_{T}\) for Run 7 Au+Au 200 GeV collisions (0-80%). Solid curves show a Breit-Wigner + linear/polynomial background function fit.

due to collisional geometry symmetry [52]. Then the \(v_{2S}\) value (i.e. \(v_{2}^{obs}\) in Eq. (5.7)) determined by the fit is corrected for the reaction plane resolution to obtain the final \(v_{2}\) of \(\phi\) meson. The final \(v_{2}\) results and related discussions will be presented in the following section 6.3.

